1.1 The web client, frontends/buzz-web

  * frontends/buzz-web/src is a file-level copy of block/buzz `desktop/src`
    at commit 4cd82f513214aad11c2b742ce7cc7c681e8e32a0 (recorded in
    frontends/buzz-web/UPSTREAM-PIN.md). That subtree has 2,421 files. The
    offline check (`npm run sync:check-offline`) reports 1,931 of them
    byte-identical to upstream and 490 modified. Every modified file is
    listed, with the reason for the change, in
    frontends/buzz-web/scripts/upstream-allowlist.json under the buckets
    `browser-only`, `product` and `upstreamable-pending`; that register is
    the statement of changes that section 4(b) of the licence asks for. The
    modifications were made by iGent AI in 2026.

  * The artwork and sounds under
    frontends/buzz-web/src/features/notifications/assets/sounds,
    frontends/buzz-web/src/features/onboarding/assets/{harness-logos,landing,starter-team}
    and
    frontends/buzz-web/src/shared/ui/assets/{app-icon@2x.png,app-icon@3x.png,pow,runtime-icons}
    are copies of block/buzz `desktop/public` files (the allowlist entries
    that carry `vendoredFrom`). frontends/buzz-web/labs-assets holds WebP
    re-encodings of some of those images, made as its README describes.
    Two of these directories carry third-party notices of their own, which
    are kept beside the files: harness-logos/CREDITS.md and pow/LICENSE.txt.

  * frontends/buzz-web/components.json, postcss.config.js, public/buzz.svg
    and public/worklet.js are unmodified upstream files. index.html,
    package.json, tailwind.config.js, tsconfig.json, tsconfig.node.json,
    preview-features.json and patches/virtua@0.49.3.patch are upstream files
    with local changes.

  * apps/buzz/src/relay/static/ui is the built output of frontends/buzz-web,
    checked in because the application embeds it. It is therefore a compiled
    form of the client above, together with the npm packages that
    frontends/buzz-web/pnpm-lock.yaml resolves, each of which is under its
    own licence.

1.2 The relay, apps/buzz

  * apps/buzz/upstream-contract/kind.rs is an unmodified copy of block/buzz
    `crates/buzz-core/src/kind.rs`. It is not compiled; it is kept so that
    the next upstream comparison has a fixed text to compare against.

  * apps/buzz/src/relay is a port of the upstream relay to Ferrite. It was
    written with upstream's Rust source open, and it is not an independent
    implementation made from the protocol documents alone. Its storage,
    admission, delivery and deployment code was written for Ferrite's
    services and has no upstream counterpart, and a line-by-line comparison
    of all of apps/buzz/src against upstream's crates finds about 290 of
    42,000 substantive lines in common. The modules below are the ones whose
    own headers say they were ported or transliterated from a named upstream
    file, or in which upstream's constants, type definitions and
    documentation comments are retained:

        src/relay/kind.rs      from crates/buzz-core/src/kind.rs
        src/relay/invites.rs   from crates/buzz-relay/src/invite_token.rs
        src/relay/audit.rs     from crates/buzz-audit (hash.rs, action.rs)
        src/relay/access.rs    from crates/buzz-relay/src/handlers/event.rs
        src/relay/filter.rs    from crates/buzz-core/src/filter.rs
        src/relay/channel.rs   from migrations/0001_initial_schema.sql and
                               crates/buzz-core/src/channel.rs

    Elsewhere in src/relay, individual functions follow a named upstream
    function closely (for example `canonical_channel_name` in
    src/relay/effects/channels.rs, and the thread, reaction, membership and
    deletion rules under src/relay/effects, whose comments cite the upstream
    function each one follows). Those portions are also derived from
    block/buzz. The rest of src/relay is iGent AI's own work.

1.3 The desktop shell, frontends/buzz-desktop

  * frontends/buzz-desktop/src-tauri/icons holds block/buzz's application
    icons, copied unmodified from `desktop/src-tauri/icons`. The shell's Rust
    code is iGent AI's own; it implements the command names and field shapes
    that the web client calls, and vendors no upstream Rust.

